menu "BuddyOS display firmware"

config BUDDY_NET
    bool "Bring up Wi-Fi and MQTT"
    default y
    help
        Off gives the face alone, which is the baseline the memory figures are
        measured against. On is the number the planning docs actually ask for.

config BUDDY_WIFI_SSID
    string "Wi-Fi SSID"
    depends on BUDDY_NET
    default ""

config BUDDY_WIFI_PASS
    string "Wi-Fi password"
    depends on BUDDY_NET
    default ""

config BUDDY_MQTT_URI
    string "MQTT broker URI"
    depends on BUDDY_NET
    default "mqtt://test.mosquitto.org:1883"
    help
        mqtt:// is plaintext. mqtts:// pulls in mbedTLS, whose handshake is the
        expensive moment — and on this product line a starved handshake has already
        been seen to misreport itself as NOT_TRUSTED rather than as out of memory.

config BUDDY_LIPSYNC_DEMO
    bool "Lip-sync the face to an embedded speech clip"
    default y
    help
        This device has no audio hardware yet, so the only way to see the mouth
        follow real speech is to play a recording through the same classifier the
        product will feed from the decoder. Costs ~75 KB of flash for 2.4 s of
        Vietnamese and English from the shipped Omnivoice voices.

        Off strips the classifier from the image entirely — which is the honest
        baseline, and also the trap: an earlier measurement here looked wonderful
        because the linker had quietly dropped the whole networking stack.

endmenu
